'''atoner''' (a̤-tō′nėr), ''n.'' One who makes atonement.

'''atones†''', ''adv.'' [Early mod. E. and ME., prop. separate, ''at ones'': now written ''at once'': see ''at'' and ''once''.] '''1.''' At once; immediately.
{{smaller block|Love me al ''atones''. {{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 94.}}}}
'''2.''' At one and the same time.
{{smaller block|Curious enditing and hard sentence is ful hevy ''atones'' for swich a child to lerne. {{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Astrolabe.}}}}

'''atonic''' (a-ton′ik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< Gr. ἄτονος, (''a'') not stretched, relaxed, languid, < ἀ- priv. + τείνειν, stretch; (''b'') without accent, < ἀ- priv. + τόνος, accent, < τείνειν, stretch: see ''a-''<sup>18</sup> and ''tonic''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' In ''pathol.'', characterized by atony, or want of tone or power: as, an ''atonic'' disease.—'''2.''' In ''philol.'': (''a'') Unaccented. (''b'') Produced by the breath alone; surd.—{{smaller|'''Atonic dyspepsia,''' defective digestion, independent of inflammation or other recognizable lesions of the digestive organs.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' In ''med.'', a drug capable of allaying organic excitement or irritation. [Rare.]—'''2.''' In ''philol.'': (''a'') A word or syllable that has no accent.
{{smaller block|A single unaccented syllable is called an ''atonic''. {{right|''F. A. March'', Anglo-Saxon Grammar, p. 222.}}}}

