'''-atory.''' [< L. ''-ātorius'', being ''-ius'' added to nouns in ''-ātor''.] A termination of adjectives, of Latin origin, in form from nouns in ''-ator'', but in sense often to be referred to the original verb, as in ''amatory'', ''accusatory'', ''declamatory'', ''exclamatory'', ''nugatory'', etc. {{smaller|When from English nouns in ''-ator'', the termination is ''-ator'' + ''-ial'', as ''senatorial'', etc.}}

'''atour<sup>1</sup>''' (ạ-tör′), ''prep.'' and ''adv.'' [Sc., also written ''attour'', ''atower'', < ME. (Scotch) ''atour'', ''atoure'', ''at-oure'', < ''at'' + ''our'', ''ower'', over: see ''at'' and ''over''; for the combination, cf. ''at-after''.] '''I.''' ''prep.'' '''1.''' Of place, over.—'''2.''' Of number or quantity, over; beyond; more than.

'''II.''' ''adv.'' Over and above; besides.—{{smaller|'''By and atour''' (prep. and adv.), also '''by atour''' (adv.), over and above. [Scotch in all uses.]}}

'''atour<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' See ''attour''<sup>2</sup>.

'''atrabilarian''' (at″rạ-bi-lā′ri-ạn), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ML. ''atrabilarius'', < L. ''atra bilis'', black bile: see ''atrabile'' and ''bile''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Affected with melancholy, which the ancients attributed to black bile; atrabilious.
{{smaller block|The ''atrabilarian'' constitution, or a black, viscous, pitchy consistence of the fluids.
{{right|''Arbuthnot'', Aliments.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A person of an atrabiliar temperament; a hypochondriac. ''Disraeli.''

'''atrabilarious''' (at″rạ-bi-lā′ri-us), ''a.'' [< ML. ''atrabilarius'': see ''atrabilarian''.] Same as ''atrabilarian''.
{{smaller block|Christopher Glowry, Esquire, . . . was naturally of an ''atrabilarious'' temperament, and much troubled with those}}

