'''atrabilariousness†''' (at″ra-bi-lā′ri-us-nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being atrabilious or melancholy.

'''atrabile†''', ''n.'' [< F. ''atrabile'', formerly ''atrebile'', = Sp. ''atrabilis'' = Pg. ''atrabilis'' = It. ''atrabile'', < L. (formerly also in E.) ''atra bilis'' (tr. Gr. μελαγχολία: see ''melancholy''), lit. black bile: ''atra'', fem. of ''ater'', black; ''bilis'', bile: see ''bile''<sup>2</sup>.] Black bile; melancholy: from the supposition that melancholy is due to a preponderance of the so-called “black bile,” an imagined secretion of the renal or atrabiliary glands.

'''atrabiliar, atrabiliary''' (at-ra-bil′i-är, -ā-ri), ''a.'' [< NL. *''atrabiliarius'', < L. ''atră bilis'', black bile: see ''atrabile''.] Melancholic or hypochondriacal; atrabilious. See ''atrabile''.

{{smaller block|Complexion of a multiplex ''atrabiliar'' character, the final shade of which may be the pale sea-green. {{right|''Carlyle'', French Rev., I. iv. 4.}}}}

'''Atrabiliary capsules, glands.''' {{smaller|See ''capsule'', ''gland''.}}

'''atrabilious''' (at-ra-bil′ius), ''a.'' [< L. ''atra bilis'': see ''atrabile'', and cf. ''bilious''.] Affected as if by black bile; melancholic or hypochondriacal; splenetic. See ''atrabile''.

{{smaller block|A hard-faced, ''atrabilious'', earnest-eyed race, stiff from long wrestling with the Lord in prayer, and who had taught Satan to dread the new Puritan hug. {{right|''Lowell'', Biglow Papers.}}}}

'''atracheate''' (a-trā′kē-āt), ''a.'' [NL. ''atracheatus'', < Gr. ἀ- priv. (''a-''<sup>18</sup>) + NL. ''trachea''.] Having no tracheæ or spiracles, as some arthropods, such as crustaceans.

